"""Original governance scenarios; all requirements and figures are hypothetical."""
ITEMS=[
('A merger leaves two control catalogs covering the same contractual requirements. Auditors cannot tell which evidence satisfies which requirement. Which governance action directly resolves this traceability gap?',
 ['Map each obligation to the controls and artifacts proving it','Name one executive owner for each merged control catalog','Retire the smaller catalog and keep the larger one','Commission an external SOC 2 Type II report covering both merged catalogs'],
 'A crosswalk from each obligation to the controls and evidence that satisfy it is what auditors need for traceability. Naming owners or keeping one catalog does not show which evidence covers which requirement, and an external report attests to controls without mapping these contracts.'),
('Two executives each assume the other can accept a critical residual risk. The mitigation work is already funded and tracked. Which governance action addresses the unresolved authority?',
 ['Record in the register who may sign off on it','Raise the critical risk to the board audit committee each month','Add a compensating control until the executives agree','Recalculate the residual score with updated likelihood data'],
 'The gap is decision rights: the register must name the person authorized to accept this risk. Monthly reporting, extra controls or a new score leave nobody empowered to make the acceptance decision.'),
('Cloud billing lists twelve production subscriptions, while the configuration database lists nine. The security team cannot establish the population for its control review. What should it do first?',
 ['Reconcile the billing and CMDB records into one authoritative list','Sample three of the nine CMDB subscriptions for testing','Enable a posture scanner only on the nine known subscriptions','Ask finance to cancel the three unmatched subscriptions'],
 'The review population must be complete before sampling or scanning, so the billing and CMDB records are reconciled first. Testing only the nine known subscriptions would leave three outside the review, and cancelling them before investigation could break production.'),
('A legacy service cannot meet a required configuration until a signed replacement contract completes. The risk owner permits temporary compensating controls, but the exception has no review boundary. What should be added?',
 ['Expiry or mandatory review date on the approved exception','Second compensating control on the same legacy service','Residual risk score recalculated with the new controls','Note in the asset inventory marking the service legacy'],
 'An exception without an expiry or review date silently becomes permanent. More controls, a new score or an inventory tag do not force the decision to be revisited when the contract completes.'),
('A company begins a CSF profile exercise. It has a list of desired capabilities but no reliable evidence of which outcomes it achieves today. Which activity supplies the missing baseline?',
 ['Documenting a Current Profile of outcomes achieved now','Drafting a Target Profile with the desired capabilities','Selecting an Implementation Tier for risk governance','Running a gap analysis between two draft profiles'],
 'A Current Profile records the outcomes the organization achieves today and is the baseline. A Target Profile describes the future, a Tier describes rigor of risk practices, and a gap analysis needs a Current Profile to compare against.'),
('A hospital has assessed its present security posture and now plans a remote-care service with new dependencies. What profile activity should capture the security outcomes needed for that future service?',
 ['Building a Target Profile that includes the new service','Rerunning the Current Profile assessment on existing systems','Moving the organization to a higher Implementation Tier','Mapping the new service only to the Recover function'],
 'A Target Profile expresses the desired outcomes, including anticipated changes such as a new service. Reassessing current systems describes today, a Tier is not a set of outcomes, and the service needs outcomes across all functions, not one.'),
('A retailer has agreed Current and Target Profiles. It has enough budget for only three of twelve differences this quarter. What is the next planning activity?',
 ['Rank the gaps by risk and build a prioritized action plan','Repeat the Current Profile to confirm the twelve differences','Fund the three cheapest gaps to close the most items','Lower the Target Profile until only three gaps remain'],
 'Risk-informed prioritization of the gaps turns the profile comparison into an action plan for scarce budget. Choosing the cheapest gaps ignores risk, and lowering the target to fit the budget hides real exposure.'),
('A business reports that every action in its CSF improvement plan is closed. Before updating the Current Profile to show the target achieved, what should the assessor do?',
 ['Test that the changes achieve the intended outcomes','Archive the closed tickets as evidence of completion','Raise the organization to the next Implementation Tier','Rebaseline the Target Profile for the next cycle'],
 'Closed tickets show work was done, not that outcomes were achieved, so the assessor tests effectiveness before updating the Current Profile. Archiving tickets or changing Tiers does not supply that evidence.'),
('Procurement is about to invite bids for a hosted payroll platform. No vendor has been selected, and bidders need comparable minimum security expectations. Which action belongs at this point?',
 ['Put baseline security clauses into the request for proposal','Run a joint incident exercise with each shortlisted bidder','Request a right-to-audit clause after contract signature','Onboard the cheapest bidder and assess it in the first year'],
 'Minimum security requirements in the RFP let proposals be evaluated on the same basis before selection. Joint exercises, post-signature clauses and first-year assessments all come too late to shape the choice.'),
('An approved cloud vendor announces that a new company will process customer records in another jurisdiction. Existing service features remain unchanged. Which action targets the new risk?',
 ['Assess the new subprocessor and its transfer arrangements','Rely on the original vendor assessment since features are unchanged','Ask the vendor to resend its current SOC 2 report','Shorten the contract renewal cycle from three years to one'],
 'A new subprocessor in another jurisdiction is a new access and transfer dependency that needs its own assessment. The original approval and the vendor\'s existing report do not cover the new party, and a shorter renewal does not assess it now.'),
('A critical outsourcing contract names internal and vendor incident contacts, but neither team has tested escalation or evidence handoff. Which activity most directly checks whether those arrangements work?',
 ['Joint tabletop exercise with the vendor response team','Contract amendment adding stricter notification hours','Annual review of the vendor incident response policy','Penetration test of the vendor customer portal'],
 'A joint exercise tests whether cross-organizational escalation and evidence handoff actually work. Tightening contract wording or reviewing a policy document does not demonstrate execution, and a penetration test examines technical exposure instead.'),
('A support agreement has ended and all required records have been returned. The supplier still has a federated support account. Which lifecycle action closes this remaining exposure?',
 ['Revoke that identity and remove the trust with their tenant','Request a certificate of data destruction from the supplier','Reduce the support account to read-only permissions','Add the supplier account to quarterly access reviews'],
 'Offboarding must remove the access path itself. A destruction certificate addresses records, and read-only rights or periodic reviews leave a credential that no longer has any business reason to exist.'),
('A risk owner reviews a low-impact exposure already within approved tolerance. Further controls would cost more than the expected benefit, and no binding requirement is unmet. Which response fits this decision?',
 ['Formally accept it and document the sign-off','Mitigate it with the least expensive available control','Transfer it through an endorsement on cyber insurance','Avoid it by retiring the affected business process'],
 'Documented acceptance fits an authorized exposure within tolerance whose controls cost more than they save. Mitigating, insuring or retiring the process would spend more than the risk justifies.'),
('A business keeps a needed remote service but deploys phishing-resistant authentication to make account takeover less likely. Which risk response describes this change?',
 ['Mitigation that lowers the likelihood of the event','Transfer of the takeover loss to the identity vendor','Acceptance of the residual takeover exposure','Avoidance by changing how the service is used'],
 'Adding a control that makes takeover less likely is mitigation. The service continues, so nothing is avoided, and the loss has not been shifted to another party.'),
('A company retains its service and existing controls but purchases coverage for specified incident-related losses. Which response describes the insurance arrangement?',
 ['Transfer of specified losses to an insurer','Mitigation that reduces how often incidents occur','Avoidance of the incident through a policy exclusion','Acceptance of residual risk recorded by the CFO'],
 'Insurance transfers specified financial consequences under its terms. It does not change incident frequency, and accountability for the incident itself stays with the company.'),
('An organization decides that a proposed biometric feature creates unacceptable residual privacy risk and removes the feature from its product plan. Which response is this?',
 ['Avoidance by dropping the activity that creates the risk','Mitigation through template encryption on the device','Transfer to the biometric sensor manufacturer','Acceptance of the risk after a privacy impact assessment'],
 'Removing the activity that creates the risk is avoidance. Encrypting templates would keep the feature with a control, which is mitigation.'),
('A board asks which of two investments yields the greater expected annual reduction in monetary loss. Reliable frequency and cost estimates are available. Which analysis best answers its question?',
 ['Compare the ALE reduction each option would deliver','Plot both risks on a five-by-five heat map','Score each investment with a maturity model','Rank the investments with a qualitative impact scale'],
 'The board wants money, and reliable frequency and cost data allow ALE comparisons that estimate each investment\'s loss reduction. Heat maps, maturity scores and qualitative ranks do not express monetary savings.'),
('An emerging threat has too little reliable frequency data for defensible monetary estimates. Stakeholders can consistently distinguish limited, serious and catastrophic mission effects. Which assessment approach fits?',
 ['Rank it on a qualitative scale with defined impact levels','Estimate annualized loss expectancy from industry averages','Run a Monte Carlo simulation on assumed frequencies','Calculate single loss expectancy from asset value alone'],
 'Without reliable frequency data, defined qualitative levels support consistent ranking without false precision. ALE, simulations or SLE built on guessed numbers would look precise but rest on invented inputs.'),
('A risk register assumes the billing service can be restored inside its agreed outage limit. Backup jobs complete, but nobody has restored the service. Which action tests the assumption?',
 ['Perform a timed recovery test in an isolated environment','Check the backup job logs for successful completion','Recalculate the outage limit from the business impact analysis','Add a second backup target in another region'],
 'Only an actual timed restore shows whether the service comes back inside the limit. Successful jobs, more copies and a recalculated limit do not prove recoverability.'),
('A mitigation owner marks an access-review finding resolved. The assessor needs assurance that excess privileges were actually removed. Which action provides the relevant evidence?',
 ['Export current entitlements and compare them with the finding','Accept the owner sign-off recorded in the ticketing system','Recalculate the risk score for the access-review finding','Schedule the next access review for the following quarter'],
 'Comparing current entitlements with the finding shows whether the excess privileges are gone. A sign-off, a new score or a future review does not verify the access state now.'),
('Counsel issues a valid legal hold covering specified investigation records. The normal deletion job will reach those records tonight. Which action should the information owner arrange?',
 ['Suspend deletion for the held records and preserve them','Let deletion run and restore the records from backup later','Copy the held records to a personal archive for safety','Delete the records on schedule and log the deletion'],
 'A legal hold overrides routine disposal, so the owner suspends deletion and preserves the records with counsel. Relying on later restores or private copies risks spoliation and breaks custody.'),
('A dataset reaches its approved retention limit. Counsel confirms there is no hold or other retention duty, and the business has no remaining authorized purpose. Which action follows lifecycle governance?',
 ['Securely dispose of it as the schedule requires','Keep the dataset indefinitely in cold storage','Anonymize half of the fields and keep the rest','Extend retention by one year as a precaution'],
 'With no hold, duty or purpose left, the approved schedule calls for disposal. Indefinite or extended retention adds exposure without a justified purpose.'),
('A team proposes using identifiable support records for unrelated model training. The records remain within the same country, but the new purpose has not been assessed or authorized. What should governance address first?',
 ['Whether this reuse is lawful and has been approved','Whether the records need a cross-border transfer mechanism','Whether the training cluster uses encryption at rest','Whether the support records exceed their retention limit'],
 'Reusing personal data for an unrelated purpose needs a purpose review and authorization first. The data stays in one country, so a transfer mechanism is not the issue, and encryption does not make an unauthorized purpose lawful.'),
('A regional service will move personal records to a foreign processor. The same processing purpose continues. Which issue needs specific review before the move?',
 ['Legal transfer mechanism for that country','Whether the processing purpose is still compatible','Retention schedule for the regional records','Data classification labels applied to each customer record'],
 'Cross-border transfers depend on the jurisdictions involved and the legal transfer mechanism. The purpose is unchanged, and retention or labels do not decide whether the move is permitted.'),
('Design reviewers cannot explain where an external analytics provider receives customer records or which identity crosses into its service. Which threat-modeling activity is missing?',
 ['Diagramming data flows and where trust changes','Brainstorming attacker goals with STRIDE','Scoring each threat with a DREAD rating','Selecting mitigations for each identified threat'],
 'A data flow diagram with trust boundaries shows what reaches the provider and which identity crosses. STRIDE, scoring and mitigations all depend on that model existing first.'),
('A team has a complete architecture model and now asks how an adversary could reach the protected transaction service through its dependencies. Which activity answers that question?',
 ['Enumerating threats and attack paths against the model','Redrawing the data flow diagram at a finer level','Assigning owners to each component in the model','Running an external vulnerability scan on the service'],
 'With the model complete, the next step is identifying threats and paths an attacker could take. Redrawing the model repeats work, owners do not find threats, and a scan sees only exposed weaknesses, not design paths.'),
('Reviewers have identified a credible attack path through a partner integration and agreed its risk is unacceptable. What threat-modeling activity should follow?',
 ['Design mitigations for that path and assign them','Re-enumerate threats for the partner integration','Accept the path because it requires partner access','Add the partner to the annual vendor questionnaire'],
 'Once a threat is judged unacceptable, the model moves to mitigation design and ownership. Repeating discovery, accepting an unacceptable risk or sending a questionnaire does not address it.'),
('A new isolation boundary has been added to close a documented threat path. Which activity determines whether the threat model can treat that path as mitigated?',
 ['Run an attack simulation against the new isolation','Mark the path closed when the change ticket merges','Update the data flow diagram to show the boundary','Lower the threat rating in the risk register'],
 'Validation tests the change against the threat before the model treats it as closed. A merged ticket, an updated diagram or a lower rating are claims, not evidence.'),
('A purchasing assistant can both recommend vendors and execute unrestricted purchases. The immediate concern is excessive agency even when its recommendations are accurate. Which safeguard most directly reduces this exposure?',
 ['Scope its tools so it can only draft orders for approval','Add a system prompt forbidding purchases above a limit','Filter its responses for prompt injection strings','Retrain it on a curated set of approved vendors'],
 'Excessive agency is fixed by limiting what tools and actions the assistant can perform. A prompt instruction can be overridden, injection filters do not cap authority, and retraining changes recommendations, not permissions.'),
('A model returns structured content that another service treats as trusted instructions. Tool permissions are already narrow, but malformed output can alter that service\'s behavior. Which boundary needs a control?',
 ['Validate model output against a strict schema before use','Remove more permissions from the model tool set','Encrypt the channel between the model and the service','Log every model response for later review'],
 'Model output is untrusted input to the downstream service and must be validated before use. Permissions are already narrow, encryption protects transit only, and logging detects problems afterwards.'),
('A model\'s behavior changes after unverified third-party records are added to its training corpus. Which process most directly addresses the suspected supply of poisoned material?',
 ['Provenance checks and review of training data sources','Human review of the model final recommendations','Rate limiting on the model inference endpoint','Output filtering for disallowed response content'],
 'Poisoning enters through training data, so provenance and source review target it. Reviewing outputs, limiting requests or filtering responses treat symptoms rather than the corrupted corpus.'),
('A benefits system gives a model\'s recommendation final authority over consequential eligibility decisions. The policy requires an accountable reviewer to assess evidence and exceptions. Which safeguard satisfies that requirement?',
 ['Trained caseworker who makes the final call on each case','Confidence threshold above which the model decides alone','Schema validation on each recommendation the model returns','Monthly sample audit of model eligibility outcomes'],
 'The policy needs an accountable person to make the decision on the evidence. A threshold still lets the model decide, schema checks only test structure, and an after-the-fact audit is not decision review.'),
('An enterprise assistant retrieves a supplier page containing instructions that conflict with its assigned task. It follows those page instructions. Which AI threat category best describes the entry path?',
 ['Indirect prompt injection','Training data poisoning of the base model','Model inversion revealing training records','Denial of service through costly queries'],
 'The hostile instructions arrived inside external content the assistant retrieved, which is indirect prompt injection. Nothing changed the training data, and no records were extracted.'),
('A protected model artifact is copied by a contractor without authorization. There is no evidence of altered training or service interruption. Which category best describes the observed loss?',
 ['Model theft, a confidentiality loss','Data poisoning, an integrity loss','Model denial of service, an availability loss','Prompt injection, a control loss'],
 'Unauthorized copying of the model weights is model theft and a confidentiality loss. Training and service were unaffected, so poisoning and denial of service do not fit.'),
('Monitoring shows deliberately expensive model requests saturating inference capacity and preventing ordinary users from receiving responses. Which category best describes the effect?',
 ['Model denial of service through resource exhaustion','Indirect prompt injection through crafted requests','Sensitive information disclosure in responses','Membership inference against the training set'],
 'Costly requests that exhaust inference capacity and block other users are a denial of service. The requests are not injecting instructions or extracting data.'),
('Before a model is trained, an attacker systematically changes labels in a trusted training dataset. Which category best describes the compromise?',
 ['Data poisoning, an integrity compromise','Direct prompt injection at inference time','Model extraction through repeated queries','Insecure handling of model output'],
 'Changing labels before training corrupts the data the model learns from, which is poisoning. Injection and extraction act on a deployed model instead.'),
('A completed control materially reduces the probability of a recorded threat, but the register still shows the pre-control exposure. Which update is needed before the next acceptance decision?',
 ['Reassess the residual risk with the control in place','Close the register entry because the control is done','Escalate the original exposure to the board again','Transfer the remaining exposure through insurance'],
 'The acceptance decision should rest on residual risk with the new control in place. Closing the entry assumes no risk remains, and escalation or transfer acts on an out-of-date figure.'),
('A project manager may accept exposures up to the company\'s moderate band. The assessed residual risk is high, and the service is still required. Which governance step is needed?',
 ['Escalate to a risk owner authorized for high risks','Accept the risk because delivery is urgent','Reclassify the risk as moderate to stay in scope','Start the service and log the risk for review'],
 'A high residual risk exceeds the project manager\'s delegated authority, so it goes to an owner who can accept it. Urgency, reclassification or starting anyway all bypass that boundary.'),
('An authorized risk owner has approved a funded mitigation plan with named owners and dates. The team has not yet applied the control. What is the immediate next step?',
 ['Implement the approved control, then validate it','Request a second approval from the steering group','Reassess the inherent risk before starting work','Update the plan dates to the next quarter'],
 'The decision is made and funded, so the team carries out the treatment and then checks that it works. A second approval or another assessment delays the risk reduction.'),
('A system has been decommissioned and its records disposed of lawfully. Review confirms that a threat entry relates only to that system, with no remaining dependencies. What should the register owner do?',
 ['Close the entry and keep its history for audit','Leave the entry open in case the system returns','Delete the entry and its history from the register','Transfer the entry to the replacement system'],
 'An obsolete entry should be closed with evidence while its history is kept for audit. Leaving it open distorts the current picture, deleting it loses the record, and the threat does not apply to the replacement.'),
('Leadership states that sensitive customer information must be protected throughout its lifecycle and assigns organizational accountability. Which documentation layer expresses this broad direction?',
 ['Policy','Standard','Procedure','Baseline'],
 'A policy states management intent and assigns accountability. Standards, procedures and guidelines support it with specific requirements, steps or advice.'),
('An organization specifies an approved minimum configuration that every managed server must meet unless an exception is granted. Which documentation type fits this mandatory baseline?',
 ['Technical standard','Security policy','Runbook procedure','Advisory guideline'],
 'A standard sets a mandatory, specific baseline that needs an exception to depart from. A guideline is advisory, and a policy states direction without technical settings.'),
('An on-call runbook identifies the ordered steps, approval checks and rollback actions for rotating a service credential. Which documentation type is this?',
 ['Operational procedure','Cryptography policy','Key length standard','Best practice guideline'],
 'Ordered steps with checks and rollback are a procedure: how to perform a task. Policies and standards state what must be done, not the sequence.'),
('A security team publishes optional examples of useful dashboard layouts. Teams may choose alternatives without requesting an exception. Which documentation type fits?',
 ['Guideline','Standard','Policy','Control objective'],
 'Optional recommendations that need no exception are guidelines. A standard is mandatory and would require an exception to depart from.'),
('A crisis exercise reveals that staff receive conflicting public statements because several departments brief the media independently. Which plan element needs focused testing?',
 ['Communications plan and approved spokespeople','Recovery order in the continuity plan','Evidence preservation procedure','Delegation of emergency shutdown authority'],
 'Conflicting public statements point to the communications plan: who speaks, through which channels and with which approved messages. Recovery order, evidence handling and shutdown authority do not govern media briefings.'),
('During a breach exercise, responders overwrite relevant records while rebuilding systems. Which plan element needs correction to support later investigation?',
 ['Evidence capture before any reimaging begins','Communications plan for regulators','Recovery time objective for the systems','Call tree for incident responders'],
 'Rebuilding before collecting evidence destroyed it, so the plan must require evidence capture before rebuild. Regulator messaging, recovery targets and call trees do not protect artifacts.'),
('A recovery drill restores a reporting service before the identity service it requires, delaying all usable access. Which planning element should be revised?',
 ['Recovery sequence based on system dependencies','Recovery point objective for the reporting data','Backup schedule for the identity service','Communications plan for affected users'],
 'Restoring a service before its dependency produces nothing usable, so the sequence must follow dependencies. Recovery points, backup schedules and user messages do not fix the order.'),
('A simulated incident occurs while the primary executive is unavailable. No participant knows who can authorize an emergency service shutdown. What governance element is missing?',
 ['Named alternates with delegated decision authority','Updated contact list for the executive team','Pre-written shutdown procedure for the service','Post-incident review of the executive absence'],
 'Named alternates with delegated authority let decisions be made when the primary is absent. A contact list or procedure does not grant the right to decide.'),
]
assert len(ITEMS)==48
